        "titel": "The social construction of reality",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "**What this chapter is about.** So far you have looked at the big picture: societies, cultures, organisations, industrialisation, the information revolution. That is **[[macro-sociology|Macro-sociology]]**, the study of large-scale society.\n\nNow the telescope is turned around. **[[Micro-sociology]]** is the study of everyday life in social interactions: how people go shopping, make breakfast, go to work, use mobile phones, make love, fight wars, lie, cheat and steal. These are the building blocks a society is actually made of.\n\n**The opening story: Anna.** On a cold winter day in 1938, a social worker investigating possible child abuse found a five-year-old girl hidden in a second-floor storage room of a Pennsylvania farmhouse. Anna was wedged into an old chair with her arms tied above her head so that she could not move, in filthy clothes, her arms and legs like matchsticks.\n\nShe had been born in 1932 to an unmarried, mentally impaired woman of 26. Enraged by his daughter’s \"illegitimate\" motherhood, the grandfather did not want the child in his house. To lessen his anger, Anna’s mother moved her to the attic, where she received little attention and just enough milk to keep her alive. There she stayed, day after day, month after month, with essentially no human contact, for five years.\n\nSociologist **Kingsley Davis (1940)** travelled to see her immediately. She was emaciated and feeble, unable to laugh, smile, speak or even show anger, completely unresponsive, as if alone in an empty world. After four years of care she learned to walk and talk in short phrases. She died on 6 August 1942, aged ten.\n\nThe lesson: **without social experience, an individual is not a social human being.** Anna was physically alive, but scarcely any capacity for thought, emotion or meaningful behaviour had developed."
