        "tekst": "How did Anna and Piri come to inhabit the social worlds they did? Through **[[socialisation|Socialisation]]**: a lifelong social experience by which individuals construct their [[personal biography|Personal biography]], assemble daily interactional rules and come to terms with the wider patterns of their culture.\n\nUnlike species whose behaviour is largely biologically set, human beings rely on social experience to learn the nuances of their culture in order to survive.\n\n**The five questions of socialisation theory:**\n\n1. **Who** is being socialised? This raises the nature of the person and ultimately the question of \"human nature\": something you are born with, or a product of your environment? The classic nature/nurture argument.\n2. **By whom?** The *[[agents of socialisation|Agents of socialisation]]*. First mothers, fathers and siblings as primary socialisers, then friends, peers, teachers, [[significant others|Significant others]] and the mass media.\n3. **How?** The socialisation theories, the mechanisms through which we become human. Two are considered here: the psychodynamic (Freud) and the interactionist (Mead).\n4. **Where?**\n5. **When?** The last two locate the life in time and place."
