        "tekst": "**Sigmund Freud (1856-1939): the unconscious.** Freud lived in Vienna at a time when most Europeans considered human behaviour biologically fixed. Trained as a physician, he turned to the study of personality and developed psychoanalysis.\n\nThe cornerstone is the **repressed unconscious**: people’s lives are partly shaped by emotional experiences, traumas and \"family romances\" that become too difficult to confront and so become hidden from the surface workings of life, while still motivating our actions. The unconscious goes deep, is repressed and cannot be easily recovered; psychoanalysis and dream analysis are two \"roads to the unconscious\". Often, then, we really do not know why we do certain things.\n\n**Life and death.** Biology, Freud held, is diffuse but important, though not in terms of the simple instincts guiding other species. Humans respond to two general drives: a basic need for pleasure and bonding, the life instincts or **eros**, and opposing it the aggressive drives, the death instincts or **thanatos**. These opposing forces operate primarily at the level of the unconscious and generate deeply rooted inner tensions."
